## Role: multi-agent engineering organization

This project uses the **3-role dispatch pattern** (orchestrator → implementer → reviewer), **dynamic workflows** that drive those roles from a script, and a SubagentStop hook that logs role runs to Serena. Solo remains the default.

### The 3 roles

- **`orchestrator`** (Opus tier recommended) — reads the request, breaks it into self-contained subtasks with acceptance criteria, dispatches to `implementer` and `reviewer`. Does NOT edit code or run tests directly.
- **`implementer`** (Sonnet tier) — executes ONE subtask at a time. Must run `build + test` green before reporting done. Fails loud on under-specified spec (Rule 12).
- **`reviewer`** (Sonnet tier) — reads the diff, applies AGENTS.md rules, reports blocker / major / minor / nit. Does NOT modify code.

### Dynamic workflows (`.claude/workflows/`)

Not the same as this file's "Workflow" section above (the phase preset chosen with `--workflow`). A dynamic workflow is a script Claude Code runs in the background; **the script decides what runs next**, not a model turn by turn (AGENTS.md Rule 5). `/review-changes` and `/fix-loop` run the roles above as workers (`agentType`); `/judge-panel` uses untyped read-only agents, so its runs do not appear in the hook log. Watch and stop runs with `/workflows`.

| Command | What it does | Agents |
|---|---|---|
| `/review-changes [base-ref]` | correctness / conventions / test-intent reviewers on the diff vs `base-ref` (default `main`), then a skeptic per lens refutes what it can't confirm | ~6 |
| `/judge-panel <question>` | 3 advocates (simplicity / risk / fit) propose, 1 judge scores and picks — or declares a tie. Read-only. Used by the `handoff` skill | 4 |
| `/fix-loop <check command>` | implementer runs the check, fixes, repeats; stops green, after 2 rounds with no progress, or at 5 checks | ≤ 9 |

The scaffold never overwrites these files — edit them to fit your project.

### Which dispatch mode

| Task shape | Mode |
|---|---|
| Single-file typo, config change, anything < 3 steps | **Solo** (main loop) |
| Known, repeatable shape — review a diff, decide between approaches, make a red check green | **Dynamic workflow** |
| One-off task whose decomposition you only learn by reading the code | **`orchestrator`** + implementers (parallel if independent, serial if they share files) + reviewer per output |
| Another live session needs to know something now | **Message it** — durable content to Serena first, the message points at it (see "Cross-session messaging" above) |

### SubagentStop hook

Registered in `.claude/settings.json` by the scaffold. Each `orchestrator` / `implementer` / `reviewer` completion appends `- <timestamp> SubagentStop <role>` to `.serena/memories/qa/agent-org-log.md`. Other subagents (Claude Code's internal agents, un-typed workflow agents) are ignored. Log-only; it never blocks.

If you want to gate on test/lint before allowing "done", extend the hook script to run `npm test` (or your project's equivalent) and `exit 2` on failure — that re-prompts the subagent to keep working. Only do this once your test suite is fast enough that a per-subagent run is acceptable.

### Cross-session messaging in this profile

- `isolatePeerMachines` is `true`: a message to a session on **another machine or in the cloud** asks you first (it travels through Anthropic's servers). Same-machine messages don't prompt.
- A session running with **bypassed permissions holds** messages from normal sessions for your approval, and drops them after 5 minutes unanswered (`dialogExpiry`).
- **WSL 2 ↔ native Windows** sessions can't reach each other; nor can a **container ↔ its host**. `--bare` sessions have no inbox.

### Cost disclosure

This pattern is expensive on the end-user's Anthropic account:

- Opus orchestrator + N Sonnet workers running concurrently; a dynamic workflow runs every agent in its table above.
- Rough back-of-envelope: a 3-role dispatch on a medium task consumes ~5–10× the tokens of solo work on the same task.
- Only worth it when task decomposition genuinely benefits from independent context (see "Which dispatch mode").

### Boundaries

- Human gates stay. Nothing here auto-merges PRs. Reviewers surface issues; humans decide.
- No swarm-for-swarm's-sake. Solo is the default; dispatch is opt-in per task.
- Requires Claude Code ≥ 2.1.248 (dynamic workflows + `agentType` roles; also covers messaging on native Windows, ≥ 2.1.234).
- The scaffold does not turn on `ultracode` or set a workflow size guideline — those are your cost choices (`/effort`, `/config`).

